=== ⑥ PSS `restricted` + ⑦ TTL sweeper honesty — live (2026-07-26, kind, $0) ===

Run in a throwaway namespace (pss-test) so the ArgoCD-managed `pa` release was
never touched. Namespace labelled:
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest

WHY BOTH DIRECTIONS
  "Our pods start" proves nothing on its own — they would also start in a
  namespace that is not enforcing anything. So the control is a deliberately
  NON-compliant install that the API server must refuse.

--- CONTROL: non-compliant install (securityContext.enabled=false) ---
Error creating: pods "pa-bad-platform-agent-webhook-589b6b6454-hw57c" is
forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":
  allowPrivilegeEscalation != false (container "webhook" must set
    securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation=false),
  unrestricted capabilities (container "webhook" must set
    securityContext.capabilities.drop=["ALL"]),
  runAsNonRoot != true (pod or container "webhook" must set
    securityContext.runAsNonRoot=true),
  seccompProfile (pod or container "webhook" must set
    securityContext.seccompProfile.type to "RuntimeDefault" or "Localhost")

  helm install FAILED, Replicas 0/1. The namespace really is enforcing, and it
  names all four violations our securityContext exists to satisfy.

--- COMPLIANT install (chart defaults) ---
NAME                                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
pa-pss-platform-agent-webhook-d9b4d6587-jm5k4   1/1     Running   0          7s

  running as: uid=10001(app) gid=10001(app) groups=10001(app)

  Admitted AND actually running as the non-root user baked into
  infra/onprem/Dockerfile (USER 10001:10001). Chart runAsUser/fsGroup and the
  image USER are now guarded against drifting apart — a mismatch there only
  surfaces at runtime, on the PVC write.

  Note this required a real image change, not just chart YAML: the image ran as
  root and had no `scripts/` tree at all.

--- ⑦ sweeper: the fix that made the CronJob safe to ship ---
The sweeper shells out to `gcloud`/`aws`/`az`. If a CLI is missing or
unauthenticated it used to yield zero clusters — indistinguishable from a
healthy empty cloud. As a CronJob in a container without those CLIs that is a
green all-clear, forever, while the clusters it exists to catch keep billing.

Run inside the newly built container (which carries no cloud CLIs):

  $ python scripts/sweep_orphan_clusters.py --provider gcp
  COVERAGE INCOMPLETE — 1 of 1 provider(s) were not inventoried.
  This report cannot mean 'clean':
    - gcp: gcloud failed: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'gcloud'
  exit code: 2

  Exit 2 = "could not evaluate", matching the handoff preflight's discipline.
  A failed Job is visible in `kubectl get jobs`; a green one that never looked
  is not. Unreachable is now louder than empty, which is the only ordering that
  makes an unattended sweeper worth having.

  Exit codes: 0 = full coverage and nothing over budget, 1 = orphans found,
  2 = coverage incomplete. Scope with --provider to earn a real green.

  Also fixed one level down: an `eks describe-cluster` that fails AFTER a
  successful list no longer discards the cluster — it keeps the row with an
  unknown creation time, which find_orphans reports rather than skips.

CronJob is default OFF (needs provider CLIs + read-only credentials this chart
does not assume) and report-only — there is no --delete flag to pass, because
destructive actions are approval-gated (D5).

--- cleanup ---
pa-pss uninstalled, pss-test namespace deleted. `pa` release untouched
throughout (ArgoCD still Synced/Healthy).

=== end ===

=== ⑥ second half — Cosign image signing (live, local kind registry, $0) ===

Setup: image pushed to the kind registry (localhost:5001), ephemeral key pair
generated in a scratch dir (never committed), `cosign sign --key`.

--- what verifies ---
  $ cosign verify --key cosign.pub localhost:5001/platform-agent:0.1.0
  - The signatures were verified against the specified public key      exit 0

--- THE FINDING: a signature covers a DIGEST, not a tag ---
The first control was wrong and the tool corrected it. Pushing the same image
under a second tag `:unsigned` — never signed by that name — still VERIFIED,
because both tags resolve to the same digest:

  localhost:5001/platform-agent:unsigned -> sha256:7cfc8a4d...  exit 0

So "this tag is signed" is not a property of the tag. The real control is a
DIFFERENT image pushed under our repo:tag:

  $ docker tag alpine:3.20 localhost:5001/platform-agent:tampered && push
  $ cosign verify --key cosign.pub localhost:5001/platform-agent:tampered
  Error: no signatures found                                           exit 1

Consequence, now in the chart: `image.digest` deploys `repo@sha256:...` and wins
over `tag`. Verifying a tag and then deploying that tag leaves a window where
the tag can be moved between the two steps; pinning the digest closes it.
Guarded by test_digest_pin_wins_over_tag (a digest-pinned ref must not also
carry the mutable tag).

--- the gate script, all three exits ---
  scripts/verify_image_signature.py

  signed, by digest      -> VERIFIED: ...@sha256:7cfc8a4d...            exit 0
  substituted image      -> NOT SIGNED: ...:tampered carries no
                            signature from this identity                exit 1
  no --key / identity    -> refused: verifying against "any signature"
                            accepts a signature from anyone             exit 2

  cosign missing also exits 2, not 0: a CI step that passes because the verifier
  was absent is worse than no step, since everything downstream then believes
  the image was checked. Same rule as the sweeper's COVERAGE INCOMPLETE and the
  handoff preflight's "could not evaluate".

NOT DONE (deliberate): admission-time enforcement. Rejecting unsigned images at
the API server needs a policy controller (sigstore policy-controller / Kyverno),
which is a new cluster dependency and belongs with Phase 2's namespace work.
What exists today is a verifiable gate for CI and for a human, not an in-cluster
guarantee — stated plainly so nobody reads more assurance into it than it has.

Local registry still holds the throwaway `:unsigned` and `:tampered` tags from
this run (dev registry, recreated by `make local-cluster`).
